/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Examine article usage before names of diseases, viruses, bacteria, and clinical medical conditions.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Infectious and Chronic Medical Pathologies:) Tj
T*
(   Standard contemporary medical and biological English utilizes ZERO article before names of) Tj
T*
(   diseases:) Tj
T*
(   - "The patient contracted tuberculosis." \(Never *the tuberculosis\).) Tj
T*
(   - "CRISPR therapies are being developed to treat sickle cell anemia.") Tj
T*
(   - "Diabetes mellitus affects glucose homeostasis.") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Viral and Bacterial Taxa vs. Clinical Syndromes:) Tj
T*
(   - Taxonomic Organism \(Capitalized binomial or abbreviated taxon\):) Tj
T*
(   - "Escherichia coli", "Mycobacterium tuberculosis", "SARS-CoV-2". \(Zero article\).) Tj
T*
(   - The Virus / The Bacterium \(Noun adjunct modifying common noun\):) Tj
T*
(   - "The SARS-CoV-2 virus", "The E. coli bacterium", "The influenza virus". \(Takes 'The' because) Tj
T*
(   'virus/bacterium' is the common head noun\).) Tj
T*
(   - Clinical Syndromes:) Tj
T*
(   - "COVID-19" \(Zero article\) vs. "The COVID-19 pandemic" \(Takes 'the' modifying 'pandemic'\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 18:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Detail the grammatical rules of 'Some' versus 'Any' in scientific hypotheses, negative findings, and controls.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Affirmative Declaratives \('Some'\):) Tj
T*
(   - Used in positive statements to denote an unspecified, positive quantity:) Tj
T*
(   - "We detected some degradation in lane 4.") Tj
T*
(   - "Some mutant colonies exhibited ampicillin resistance.") Tj
T*
(   - Used in polite offers or affirmative inquiries expecting assent:) Tj
T*
(   - "Would you like some additional lysis buffer?") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   2. Non-Affirmative Contexts \('Any'\):) Tj
T*
(   - Used in negative sentences, conditional 'if'-clauses, and open questions:) Tj
T*
(   - Negative finding: "The negative control did not produce any fluorescent signal.") Tj
T*
(   - Conditional: "If you detect any precipitation, re-heat the mixture to 37 deg C.") Tj
T*
(   - Question: "Did the spectrophotometer register any absorbance at 260 nm?") Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(   3. 'Any' in Affirmative Clauses \(Meaning 'No Matter Which'\):) Tj
T*
(   - When 'any' is stressed in an affirmative statement, it signifies unrestricted free choice:) Tj
T*
(   - "Any calibrated micropipette can dispense this volume." \(It does not matter which one you) Tj
T*
(   select\).) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
(--------------------------------------------------------------------------------) Tj
T*
() Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F2 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(>> Solution to Question 19:) Tj
T*
ET
BT
/F1 8.5 Tf
12.5 TL
(   [Problem Statement]: Analyze the use of articles with chemical elements, compounds, functional groups, and molecular complexes.) Tj
T*
(   [Detailed Step-by-Step Answer]:) Tj
T*
(   1. Elemental Substances and Pure Compounds:) Tj
T*
ET

